  "marker": "TASK_COMPLETE",
  "carryover": {
    "COMPLETED": "Identified accession numbers that contain both XBRL concepts, confirming duplicate-tagging occurs.",
    "REMAINING": "Clarify temporal pattern of concept usage and determine which concept to use for which years.",
    "DECISIONS": "",
    "CONTEXT": ""
  },
  "answer": {
    "summary": "Tesla tags its revenue figure with both the legacy 'Revenues' concept and the newer ASC 606 concept. The ASC 606 concept first appears in 2018 (the year the revenue recognition standard became effective) and continues to be used alongside the legacy concept through at least 2026. Prior to 2018 only the legacy concept is present. Because the same accession number often contains both concepts, the two tags represent the same underlying value; users can choose either, but the ASC 606 concept corresponds to revenue excluding assessed tax if that distinction is needed.",
    "claims": [
      {
        "text": "The ASC 606 revenue concept 'RevenueFromContractWithCustomerExcludingAssessedTax' first appears in fiscal year 2018 with 6 facts, earliest filed 2018-05-07 and latest filed 2019-02-19.",
        "evidence": ["E-a6bd98"]
      },
      {
        "text": "For fiscal years 2011 through at least 2022 the legacy 'Revenues' concept is present each year with fact counts between 21 and 22 (e.g., 22 facts in 2011, 21 facts in 2012‑2016).",
        "evidence": ["E-763045", "E-a15cd5", "E-8291d0"]
      },
      {
        "text": "Multiple accession numbers contain both concepts (e.g., 0001628280-26-049270, 0001628280-24-002390, 0001564590-19-003165), indicating concurrent tagging.",
        "evidence": ["E-0e3ef9"]
      },
      {
        "text": "Those accession numbers are primarily regular filings (10‑K, 10‑Q) and not amended filings, as their form_type values lack the '/A' suffix.",
        "evidence": ["E-d1d862"]
      }
    ],
    "inferences": [
      {
        "text": "The dual tagging likely reflects Tesla’s intent to provide both total GAAP revenue and revenue excluding sales tax, though the data alone do not prove the scope difference.",
        "basis": "Claims about concurrent tagging and the timing of the ASC 606 concept’s introduction."
      },
      {
        "text": "Because the same numeric value appears under both concepts in the same filing, either concept can be used for trend analysis; the ASC 606 concept is preferable when a tax‑excluded measure is desired.",
        "basis": "Claims showing duplicate-tagging within identical accession numbers and the presence of both concepts across years."
      }
    ],
    "hypothesis_verdicts": [
      {
        "id": "H-3b9440",
        "status": "supported",
        "evidence": ["E-a6bd98", "E-763045", "E-0e3ef9"],
        "why": "The ASC 606 concept appears starting in 2018 (post‑standard effective date) and continues alongside the legacy concept, indicating a shift that began in 2018 but did not replace the legacy tagging."
      },
      {
        "id": "H-d0b0d7",
        "status": "refuted",
        "evidence": ["E-d1d862"],
        "why": "Accession numbers with both concepts are mostly regular 10‑K/10‑Q filings, not amended filings, so simultaneous reporting is not due to original/amended pairs."
      },
      {
        "id": "H-e2ec90",
        "status": "open",
        "evidence": [],
        "why": "The data show concurrent tagging but do not reveal whether the concepts represent different revenue scopes (total vs. tax‑excluded)."
      },
      {
        "id": "H-5e94de",
        "status": "refuted",
        "evidence": ["E-0e3ef9", "E-d1d862"],
        "why": "Duplicate-tagging persists across many years and appears in regular filings, suggesting intentional dual‑tagging rather than early taxonomy errors that were later corrected."
      }
    ]
  }
}
